{
  "about": [
    "The squad's positions for Monte Carlo tree search (T87): three questions of the XCOM-style mode, and what MCTS makes of each after a fixed number of iterations from a fixed seed.",
    "A position is the board as the side to move believes it -- every piece `[id, side, kind, column, row, health, movement left]`, side 0 red and 1 blue, in the board's order -- the turn's number, the piece about to act, whether it is to `walk` or to `shoot`, and the pieces of its side still to act after it.",
    "`visits` is the root of the tree after `iterations` iterations from `seed`: every answer, in the game's order, with its visits. An answer is written `1 holds`, `1 walks to 5,3 for 4` (the piece, the cell, then what the walk costs) or `3 shoots 7` (the shooter, then the target). `decision` is the most visited.",
    "The visits were not worked out by hand: no person can play out hundreds of random games. They were written from one run and are held equal in both languages -- `make test-squad-search-vectors` runs `godot/game/squad/squad_search_game.gd` and `python/gameaikit/squad/search_game.py` on the same positions with the same `Xorshift`, and one draw out of place moves every count. `map` is the tactical map's; the Godot runner refuses a copy that has drifted from it.",
    "The search's own numbers -- the exploration constant, the playout limit -- are `rules.json`'s `search`."
  ],
